<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Moravian Church</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Unitas Fratrum, Moravian Brethren</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Moravian Church, or Unitas Fratrum, is a Protestant church descended from the Bohemian Brethren of the 15th century. Refugees from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> renewed it in 1722 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herrnhut">Herrnhut</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, on the estate of Count Nicolaus von Zinzendorf. Known for communal settlements, hymn singing and overseas missions, the Brethren were welcomed by tolerant German rulers such as the counts of Wied-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuwied">Neuwied</a> and Isenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budingen">Büdingen</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moravian-church.jpg" alt="Moravian Church" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: JJackman · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AgnusDeiWindow.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Moravian Church traces its heritage to 1457 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lands-of-the-bohemian-crown">Lands of the Bohemian Crown</a>, which included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> and Silesia. Its roots lie in the Hussite movement begun by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-hus">Jan Hus</a> in early 15th-century Bohemia. Hus objected to some practices and doctrines of the Roman Catholic Church: he wanted the liturgy in Czech, priests allowed to marry, and indulgences and the idea of Purgatory done away with. Because these actions predate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation</a> by a century, some historians claim the Moravian Church was the first Protestant church.</p><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> rulers the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a> pressed on the Protestants of the Bohemian lands. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> were invited to found Roman Catholic schools, among them the university in the Moravian capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olomouc">Olomouc</a>, and in 1582 they forced the closure of local Protestant schools.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1722 a small group of the Bohemian Brethren, called the Hidden Seed, arrived at the Berthelsdorf estate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikolaus-ludwig-von-zinzendorf">Nikolaus Ludwig von Zinzendorf</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>. They had lived in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> for nearly a hundred years as an illegal underground remnant in the Catholic setting of the Habsburg Empire. Their leader, the itinerant carpenter Christian David, asked that they be allowed to settle on Zinzendorf's lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-lusatia">Upper Lusatia</a>. Zinzendorf, a nobleman raised in the traditions of Pietistic Lutheranism, agreed out of a personal commitment to helping the poor and needy. The community they founded was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herrnhut">Herrnhut</a>, which gave the church its German name, Herrnhuter Brüdergemeine.</p><p>The church soon became a missionary movement. Together with the Royal Danish Mission College, its missionaries formed the first large-scale Protestant missionary effort, and the first were sent out when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herrnhut">Herrnhut</a> had only 300 inhabitants. Within thirty years hundreds had gone to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, North and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, the Arctic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and the Far East. The Moravians were the first to send lay people as missionaries and the first Protestant denomination to minister to slaves, though some of their communities also owned slaves.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The church teaches the necessity of the New Birth, a personal conversion to Christ, together with piety, evangelism and good works. Its emblem is the Lamb of God with the flag of victory, and its motto runs: In essentials, unity; in nonessentials, liberty; and in all things, love. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishops</a> serve as spiritual leaders rather than administrators, and the church holds that it has preserved apostolic succession.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Moravian Church” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Religion shaped society deeply: the Hussite revolution transferred much church land to nobles and towns, and for two centuries Utraquists, who gave communion in both kinds, coexisted with Catholics, later joined by the <b>Unity of the Brethren</b> and Lutherans.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The worldwide <b>Moravian Church</b> takes its name from Brethren refugees who left Moravia for Herrnhut in Saxony in 1722; its missionaries reached the Caribbean by 1732.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-moravia">Margraviate of Moravia · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>…Zinzendorf, a Saxon noble who had studied under the Pietist leader August Hermann Francke, invited members of the <b>Moravian Church</b> to his estates in 1722, where they founded a community at Herrnhut; it became a refuge for other Protestants as well, and Moravian societies were later established in…</blockquote><a href="/movement/first-great-awakening">First Great Awakening · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>They formed two parties from the beginning, with a smaller number withdrawing from both around the pacifist Petr Chelčický, whose teachings would form the foundation of the <b>Unitas Fratrum</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/hussites">Hussites · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1712 Count Ernst Casimir issued an edict of tolerance that drew Inspirationists, <b>Moravian Brethren</b> and other dissenters to the Ronneburg and Marienborn.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-isenburg-budingen">County of Isenburg-Büdingen</a></li><li><blockquote>Neuwied welcomed Mennonites, <b>Moravian Brethren</b>, Catholics, Jews and Reformed Christians, each with its own congregation.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-wied-neuwied">County of Wied-Neuwied</a></li></ul></section>
